<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><h2><b>How to Build a Concrete Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<p>Before anything else, it’s worth clearing up what “concrete paver” actually means, since it gets confused fairly often with a poured concrete walkway. A concrete paver walkway is built from individual, pre-manufactured concrete units laid in a pattern over a compacted base, similar in concept to brick or natural stone. It is not one continuous poured slab. That distinction matters, because it means concrete pavers get all the practical benefits of a modular system, individual repair, design flexibility, better freeze-thaw performance, that a single poured slab simply doesn’t offer.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, concrete pavers are the most common material we install for walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, largely because they balance cost, durability, and design flexibility better than most alternatives. This guide covers the different types of concrete pavers available, how they compare to poured concrete and brick, and the full step by step process for building a concrete paver walkway that holds up long term.</p>
<h2><b>Concrete Pavers Versus Poured Concrete Versus Brick</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Concrete Pavers</b></td><td><b>Poured Concrete</b></td><td><b>Brick</b></td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Individual units, modular</td><td>One continuous slab</td><td>Individual units, modular</td></tr><tr><td>Repair approach</td><td>Lift and replace individual pieces</td><td>Often requires cutting and patching, or full replacement</td><td>Lift and replace individual pieces</td></tr><tr><td>Freeze-thaw performance</td><td>Strong, joints allow flexibility</td><td>Prone to cracking from ground movement</td><td>Good, but more porous than concrete</td></tr><tr><td>Design flexibility</td><td>High, many colors, textures, patterns</td><td>Limited, mainly color and stamped texture</td><td>Moderate, mostly limited to size and pattern</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Generally lower upfront</td><td>Generally higher</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The modular nature of concrete pavers is really the headline advantage. A poured concrete slab that cracks due to ground movement or freeze-thaw cycling typically needs to be cut out and patched, which rarely blends seamlessly with the surrounding surface, or replaced entirely in more serious cases. A concrete paver walkway that develops an issue in one spot can usually be fixed by lifting the affected pavers, correcting the base underneath, and resetting them, without touching the rest of the walkway at all.</p>
<h2><b>Types of Concrete Pavers</b></h2>
<h3><b>Standard Concrete Pavers</b></h3>
<p>The most common type, manufactured in a mold and cured to a consistent size, shape, and color throughout the batch. These are widely available, cost-effective, and work well with almost any pattern, from simple running bond to more intricate herringbone designs.</p>
<h3><b>Tumbled or Textured Concrete Pavers</b></h3>
<p>These go through an additional manufacturing process that rounds the edges and adds surface texture, giving them a softer, more weathered look that mimics natural stone or aged brick. They’re a popular choice for homeowners wanting a traditional or old-world aesthetic without the higher cost of natural stone.</p>
<h3><b>Color-Through Versus Color-Coated Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Color-through pavers have color mixed into the concrete itself throughout the entire unit, meaning the color stays consistent even if the surface wears or chips over time. Color-coated pavers have a color layer applied to the surface only, which costs less upfront but can show wear, fading, or chipping that reveals the plain gray concrete underneath after enough years of foot traffic and weather exposure.</p>
<h3><b>Permeable Concrete Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Permeable pavers are designed with wider joints and a specialized base system that allows water to drain through the walkway surface itself, rather than running off. These are less common for standard walkways than for driveways or larger paved areas, but they’re worth considering in yards with drainage challenges, since they reduce runoff and can help manage water in problem areas of a property.</p>
<h3><b>Stamped-Look Concrete Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Some concrete paver lines are manufactured to mimic the look of stamped poured concrete or natural stone, offering a similar visual style to those options while retaining the modular, repairable benefits of a true paver system.</p>
<h2><b>What You’ll Need</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Concrete pavers in your chosen type, style, and pattern, plus 5 to 10 percent extra for cuts</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, 4 to 6 inches deep depending on soil type</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Edge restraints and spikes</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>A<a href="/blog/choose-the-right-plate-compactor-size-for-a-patio-or-driveway/"> properly sized plate compactor</a></li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>4 foot level</li><li>Paver saw or angle grinder with a masonry blade</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>

