<h2><b>Step by Step: Building a Concrete Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Choose Your Paver Type and Pattern</b></h3>
<p>Decide on standard, tumbled, color-through, or another concrete paver style based on the look you’re going for and your budget, then choose a laying pattern that suits both the paver size and the walkway’s overall shape. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> walks through pattern options like herringbone, running bond, and basketweave in more depth.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Mark Your Layout and Excavate</b></h3>
<p>Mark the walkway’s width and path using stakes and string, then excavate to a depth accounting for base, sand, and paver thickness combined, generally 7 to 9 inches total. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-level-ground-for-a-paver-walkway/"> leveling ground for a paver walkway</a> covers the tools and techniques for getting this stage properly level before moving forward.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Build and Compact the Gravel Base</b></h3>
<p>Add crushed gravel in 2 inch layers, compacting thoroughly after each one. Base depth should generally run 4 to 6 inches depending on your soil type, with clay-heavy soil, common in parts of the Denver area, benefiting from the fuller 6 inch depth to handle seasonal ground movement.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Add Landscape Fabric and Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Roll landscape fabric over the compacted base to control weed growth without blocking drainage, then add and screed about 1 inch of coarse sand to create a level surface for the pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Lay the Pavers Following Your Pattern</b></h3>
<p>Starting from a fixed, straight edge, place pavers snugly against each other, tapping each into position with a rubber mallet. Check level every few rows using your 4 foot level, adjusting sand underneath individual pavers as needed rather than waiting until a whole section is finished.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Cut Border Pavers Last</b></h3>
<p>Once the main field is laid, measure and cut border pavers individually to fit the remaining gaps, rather than pre-cutting a batch based on estimated measurements.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Install Edge Restraints</b></h3>
<p>Secure edge restraints tightly against the outer row of pavers, anchoring them with spikes. This step keeps the entire pattern locked in place against seasonal ground movement and prevents gradual spreading over time.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Fill Joints With Polymeric Sand</b></h3>
<p>Sweep polymeric sand into the joints until fully packed, remove excess sand from the surface, then lightly mist the area to activate the sand’s binding properties.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Final Compaction</b></h3>
<p>Run the plate compactor over the finished walkway one final time to set everything firmly in place, then inspect for any pavers sitting higher or lower than their neighbors, adjusting as needed.</p>
<h2><b>Choosing Between Color-Through and Color-Coated Pavers for Longevity</b></h2>
<p>If long-term appearance matters to you, and it usually does once a walkway has seen a few years of foot traffic and weather, color-through pavers are generally worth the modest price premium over color-coated ones. Since color runs through the entire paver rather than sitting only on the surface, minor surface wear, scuffing, or light chipping doesn’t reveal a mismatched gray concrete underneath the way it eventually can with color-coated products.</p>
<h2><b>Should You Consider Permeable Pavers for Your Walkway</b></h2>
<p>Permeable pavers aren’t the default choice for most residential walkways, but they’re genuinely worth considering if your yard has recurring drainage problems, a low spot that collects water, or if you’re trying to reduce runoff toward a foundation or neighboring property. The wider joints and specialized base system let water pass through the walkway surface itself rather than running off toward a single drainage point, which can meaningfully improve how a problem area handles heavy rain or snowmelt.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes When Building a Concrete Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<ul><li>Choosing color-coated pavers for a high-traffic area where surface wear will show quickly</li><li>Skipping proper base depth adjustment for clay-heavy or poorly draining soil</li><li>Mixing pavers from different production batches, leading to subtle color inconsistency</li><li>Not considering permeable pavers in a yard with known drainage issues</li><li>Rushing the final compaction step, which is what actually locks the joints and base together</li></ul>
<h2><b>How Concrete Pavers Compare on Cost</b></h2>
<p>Concrete pavers generally fall in the middle of the cost spectrum, more expensive than a basic poured concrete slab but typically less expensive than brick or natural stone for a comparable walkway. Tumbled, color-through, and permeable products all add some cost above standard concrete pavers, reflecting the additional manufacturing steps or specialized materials involved. Our<a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-paver-walkway-cost/"> paver walkway cost guide</a> covers general per square foot pricing that applies broadly across concrete paver projects.</p>
<h2><b>When It’s Worth Hiring a Professional</b></h2>
<p>Concrete pavers are one of the more DIY-friendly paver materials thanks to their consistent sizing and predictable cutting behavior, but a few situations still make professional installation the safer choice, longer walkways, complex patterns like herringbone, sloped or uneven sites, or projects incorporating permeable paver systems, which require a specialized base design most DIYers haven’t worked with before.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of installing concrete paver walkways across a wide range of styles and site conditions in the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design can help you choose the right paver type for your goals and install it to hold up for decades. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see examples of the range of concrete paver styles we’ve built.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Is a concrete paver walkway the same as a poured concrete walkway?</b></h3>
<p>No. A concrete paver walkway is built from individual, pre-manufactured units laid over a compacted base, while a poured concrete walkway is one continuous slab poured on site.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the difference between color-through and color-coated concrete pavers?</b></h3>
<p>Color-through pavers have color mixed throughout the entire unit, so it stays consistent even with surface wear. Color-coated pavers have color applied only to the surface, which can wear away and reveal the plain concrete underneath over time.</p>
<h3><b>Are permeable concrete pavers worth it for a walkway?</b></h3>
<p>They’re worth considering specifically in yards with drainage challenges, since they allow water to pass through the surface rather than running off, though they aren’t necessary for most standard walkway applications.</p>
<h3><b>Do concrete pavers cost more than poured concrete?</b></h3>
<p>Generally yes, upfront. However, concrete pavers offer easier, less visible repairs and better long-term freeze-thaw performance, which can offset some of that initial cost difference over the life of the walkway.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the most durable type of concrete paver?</b></h3>
<p>Standard and color-through pavers both offer strong durability. Tumbled pavers are similarly durable but have a softer, more textured surface that some homeowners prefer aesthetically.</p>
<h3><b>Can I mix different concrete paver types in one walkway?</b></h3>
<p>It’s possible but should be done intentionally as part of a clear design plan, such as a tumbled field with a color-through border, rather than mixing products without a specific design reason.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Build a Concrete Paver Walkway That Lasts</b></h2>
<p>Concrete pavers offer a strong balance of cost, durability, and design flexibility, but choosing the right type and getting the base and installation right make the real difference in how it holds up over time.</p>
